1|0@startrek^1440543648#cufmrmw:supercold1$:Everyone who says his movies "aren't Star Trek" are simply looking at the past with rose-colored glasses. Wrath of Khan was the original "dumbing down" of the franchise in order to give it mass appeal, and everyone here seems to forget this. And, boy, was it dumb. There are huge leaps of logic, terrible plot devices, not to mention the fact that Khan comes off as a complete idiot. So, you're trying to tell me, that even though he knows where Khan is and how dangerous he is, Chekhov is going to lead them right into the Ceti-Alpha system, where he was marooned? And then they weren't smart enough to notice a whole planet was missing, having mysteriously exploded, and that they couldn't tell Ceti-Alpha 5 from Cet-Alpha 6? All for an experiment that could have been conducted virtually anywhere in the galaxy? Please. And then there's the subject of said expirement; The Genesis Device. "Horribly Contrived Plot Device" more like. It's based on no real science whatsoever, theoretical, sketchy, or otherwise, and uses phony terminology, like what the fuck is a "life matrix"? It's magic, it might as well be the Force. And then, Khan himself is laughably incompetent throughout the film. The best thing he had going for him were those brain-bugs, and that was just a luck-out. The worst scene is when he's listening in on the conversation between Kirk and Spock, and they practically spell out their little secret "By The Book" code for him. Yet, Khan is such a giant retard, that it went right over his head. Superior intellect indeed. Face it, all he does is quote Moby Dick for most of the movie, and stick his chest out. Star Trek '09, on the other hand, is *virtually the same movie* as Wrath of Khan, except done a whole lot better. It's Wrath of Khan, except for Spock instead of Kirk. All the performances fomr the new cast were spot on. And the fact that they managed to brilliantly and successfully weave a great origin story into it made it all the better. Well, mainly great. I felt Kirk driving the car off the cliff as a kid was a little much, but everything else was good. The villain, Nero, is also much better. I believed in him so much more than Khan. i believed in his power, I believed in his ferocity, I believed in his vendetta, and I believed in his plan. True, he also lucked out with the brain-bugs, but this guy isn't pretending to be some super smart ass. And what of "Red Matter", you may ask? Isn;t that just as contrived as the "Genesis Device". True it is just as fake, although I can imagine it being some sort of collection of Bozon particles that imparts properties, such as gravity, to the matter it comes in contact with, but i digress. It's just as fake as the Genesis Device, but it's not presented to us with the same pretense that the Genesis Device did. It's not trying to pass itself off as some philosophical, thought provoking thing, when, in reality, it's just a way for the villain to destroy a planet. And then we get to the one that seems to be the most divisive, Into Darkness. Even here, I think to many people went into this predisposed to enjoying the sounds of their own scoffs and eye-rolls to really watch the movie for what it was. Khan in this movie is very much an allegorical Osama Bin Laden. Admiral Marcus awoke him to help Starfleet with the fight against the Klingons, which wasn't a matter of "if" but "when" for Marcus. But Khan went turncoat and committed terrorist acts. Our own military and CIA worked with and trained Osama Bin Laden and many like him for the fight against the Russians, and we all know what he would later be responsible for. It is for this reason that I think ST:IT is very much like STVI: The Undiscovered Country. They're both allegories for modern day world events. STVI was about the wall falling in space, ST:IT was about Osama Bin Laden in space. I'll be the first to admit that the movie might have a pacing problem, and Carol Marcus was unnecessary and could have been written out entirely, but otherwise i think it accomplished what it set out to do. With the last two Star Trek movies, JJ was out to emulate the most popular of the old Star Trek movies; Wrath of Khan and Undiscovered country. But there is a 3rd they've yet to tackle: Voyage Home. Voyage Home was a lot like The Motionless Picture, except done well. It was a pure science fiction puzzle that they had to solve, and there was no villain. My hope was that Beyond would do something similar, except it seems we're getting another contemporary villain in Idris Elba. I like Elba a lot, and i know I'll like him in this movie, but i was hoping we'd move away from the whole villain trope. Still, from what I've seen, there's going to be a much greater emphasis on the frontier and exploration of the unknown. 
1|0@startrek^1440546182#cufo92j:NeverDoubt1$:Very much disagree with you regarding Wrath of Khan. However, you make a great point about Voyage Home. And it was one of the most financially successful Trek films of all time. Why not try something that's more Science Fiction and less CGI explosion fest?
