6|1@startrek^1440467087#cuelv34:Dontreadmybook$:If by Style and charisma you mean inconsistancy and a complete ignorance to what makes Star Trek special then sure. There is a reason why fans of Star Trek have always been considered "Nerds", simply put, it is because nerds are the only folks smart enough to get it. All JJ did was take Star Trek, dumb it down for the masses and asses and throw away the soul that made ST special. Gene is likely rolling over in his grave right now. And the even more fucking annoying part of all this is that there are some folks out there that are new to the franchise that actually think that this is really Star Trek. Newsflash- it isnt. It is nothing more than a bad remake of the real deal with sexy cast and absolutely zero substance. Khan is a prime example - did JJ even watch the origins of Khan? I for one am happy that anyone else is working the franchise now. And frankly, I hope this line of reboots ends soon as this cast is nothing but a bad impersonation of something that I as Trek fan hold very dear to me. 
2|0@startrek^1440487611#cueuq3g:Ashmodai20$:Whoa whoa whoa. Karl Urban was a good Dr McCoy. I mean nobody is as good as DeForest Kelly, but Urban did an amazing job. Zachary Quinto could have been a good Spock if he was written correctly. 
2|0@startrek^1440470392#cuentn8:estranged_quark$:&gt;And the even more fucking annoying part of all this is that there are some folks out there that are new to the franchise that actually think that this is really Star Trek. You hit the nail on the head here. I've known a few people who got really interested in Star Trek after watching the reboots, and decided they should watch the TV series. They thought it would similar to the films, but instead they were hit with a slowed-paced, intellectual drama, and they quickly lost interest. It even reinforced their notion that Star Trek was "nerdy," because the contrast between the TV series and the reboots was huge. I get that JJ was trying to make Star Trek more hip and fresh. The reboots are fun, but they don't deserve to carry the name 'Star Trek'.
1|0@startrek^1440520488#cuf7o2h:Freakears$:&gt; I've known a few people who got really interested in Star Trek after watching the reboots, and decided they should watch the TV series. They thought it would similar to the films, but instead they were hit with a slowed-paced, intellectual drama, and they quickly lost interest. Funny you should mention. I got into Star Trek by watching the reboots. Then I started watching the shows and original movies and loved those.
3|0@startrek^1440469276#cuen6w6:Darkspine84$:I know that the new Star Trek films get a lot of hate, but the simple fact is that Star Trek as a franchise needed them. Sure they were dumbed down action flicks, but that's what a mainstream audience wants and Star Trek needed to get more people engaged in the universe. I've seen it many times: people seeing these new films and wanting to get into the older stuff and asking questions about where to start. This community specifically has been great at picking out a good jumping off point for these new fans and welcoming them. The new films might not be your Trek or even my Trek, but they are someone's s Trek and if it gets people interacting and understanding what the main overall points of Star Trek is, then it's a win in my books. The more people with an interest in Star Trek, the more chance we have in getting a new TV show, which can afford to spend the time on the more subtle nuisances of Star Trek. To condense genuine Star Trek into two hours with broad audience appeal is an almost impossible task as demonstrated by "action Picard" in the TNG films. Let the new films do their job and get bums on seats, the rest will follow and that'll be good for all of us. I'm not gonna lie, I've had a couple of beers and this post ended up super long. I hope it made some sense and wasn't too rant-ish. 
