0|0@startrek^1440544062#cufn0dw:supercold1$:&gt;Wrath of Khan managed to bring in non Trekkies, and it's a far more intelligent movie than either of Abrams. What a complete joke. Wrath of Khan was the original "dumbing down" of the franchise in order to give it mass appeal, and everyone here seems to forget this. And, boy, was it dumb. There are huge leaps of logic, terrible plot devices, not to mention the fact that Khan comes off as a complete idiot. So, you're trying to tell me, that even though he knows where Khan is and how dangerous he is, Chekhov is going to lead them right into the Ceti-Alpha system, where he was marooned? And then they weren't smart enough to notice a whole planet was missing, having mysteriously exploded, and that they couldn't tell Ceti-Alpha 5 from Cet-Alpha 6? All for an experiment that could have been conducted virtually anywhere in the galaxy? Please. And then there's the subject of said expirement; The Genesis Device. "Horribly Contrived Plot Device" more like. It's based on no real science whatsoever, theoretical, sketchy, or otherwise, and uses phony terminology, like what the fuck is a "life matrix"? It's magic, it might as well be the Force. And then, Khan himself is laughably incompetent throughout the film. The best thing he had going for him were those brain-bugs, and that was just a luck-out. The worst scene is when he's listening in on the conversation between Kirk and Spock, and they practically spell out their little secret "By The Book" code for him. Yet, Khan is such a giant retard, that it went right over his head. Superior intellect indeed. Face it, all he does is quote Moby Dick for most of the movie, and stick his chest out. Star Trek '09, on the other hand, is virtually the same movie as Wrath of Khan, except done a whole lot better. It's Wrath of Khan, except for Spock instead of Kirk. All the performances from the new cast were spot on. And the fact that they managed to brilliantly and successfully weave a great origin story into it made it all the better. Well, mainly great. I felt Kirk driving the car off the cliff as a kid was a little much, but everything else was good. The villain, Nero, is also much better. I believed in him so much more than Khan. i believed in his power, I believed in his ferocity, I believed in his vendetta, and I believed in his plan. True, he also lucked out with the brain-bugs, but this guy isn't pretending to be some super smart ass. And what of "Red Matter", you may ask? Isn;t that just as contrived as the "Genesis Device"? True it is just as fake, although I can imagine it being some sort of collection of Bozon particles that imparts properties, such as gravity, to the matter it comes in contact with, but i digress. It's just as fake as the Genesis Device, but it's not presented to us with the same pretense that the Genesis Device did. It's not trying to pass itself off as some philosophical, thought provoking thing, when, in reality, it's just a way for the villain to destroy a planet.
1|0@startrek^1440544283#cufn523:NeverDoubt1$:You're entitled to your opinion and I'm glad you enjoyed Into Darkness. Wrath of Khan isn't about plot points. It's about the relationships between characters we've come to know. It has complex and nuanced conversations, references, and themes. It's about growing up and facing regrets. Into Darkness didn't earn any of the moments between characters because we've barely gotten to know them. The same scenes that are so moving in WoK become bizarre parody in Into Darkness, with zero stakes. I get that it's an allegory of sorts about post 9/11, but a lot of time is wasted on over the top action scenes rather than meaningful discussions or connections between people.
0|1@startrek^1440470432#cuenuhn:drgath$:&gt; I know that the new Star Trek films get a lot of hate, but the simple fact is that Star Trek as a franchise needed them. The new films get a lot of hate from a very vocal minority. They are some of the highest rated ([RT](http://www.rottentomatoes.com/search/?search=star+trek), [IMDB](http://www.imdb.com/find?ref_=nv_sr_fn&amp;q=star+trek&amp;s=all)), and highest grossing films ([BOM](http://www.boxofficemojo.com/search/?q=star%20trek)) in the franchise, which is why the Star Trek isn't dead right now. I really wish /r/startrek/ could focus more on the positives and stop regurgitating the same juvenile hate-filled comments every time someone mentions anything about the new movies. We get it, it wasn't your ideal Trek. Cool. Know what wasn't my ideal Trek? ST 5, 9, 10, Voyager, and quite a bit of TOS. But I still respect and enjoy that stuff, just slightly less than the rest, and don't go out of my way to bash it. Nice post.
3|0@startrek^1440509245#cuf0wwu:NeverDoubt1$:Just because the movies made money doesn't mean the franchise is alive and well. There are other factors besides money.
